# 0.6.4 - `vecGet` REFUSES an out-of-range index instead of answering 0.
#
# NOT A SIGNATURE CHANGE, so `verify-compat.py` cannot see it: the type
# is `(-> Int Int Int)` before and after, and the row's `#effects=` does
# not move either, because a trap is not an effect. It is recorded here
# because it is a BEHAVIOUR a caller could depend on, which is what this
# file is for - `docs/compatibility.md`'s policy is about what callers
# rely on, not about what the census can compute.
#
# THE FIRST SENTENCE STOPPED BEING TRUE LATER IN THIS SAME RELEASE, and
# it is left standing because it is why this row was written. The `Vec`
# port below retyped `vecGet` to `(-> (Vec a) Int a)`, so the census
# DOES see it now and reports `CHANGED`. This row satisfies that too -
# the permit is keyed on the kind letter and the name, not on the
# reason - but the two changes are separate events with one line: the
# trap landed first and alone, and the retype is what section 4 said
# the trap had to precede.
#
# WHAT A CALLER RELIED ON. `vecGet v i` past the end answered 0, and 0
# is a value a caller may have pushed, so a read past the end and a
# stored zero arrived identically. `stdlib/Vec.ax`'s own comment called
# that "the worse of the two failure modes: not a crash, a wrong answer
# that keeps going", and `tests/stdlib/313-vec-try.ax` asserted the
# confusion deliberately so that a change here could not pass quietly.
# It did not: 313 and `070-vec` both went red and both were rewritten.
#
# WHAT TO WRITE INSTEAD. `vecTry v i` answers `(Option a)` and is the
# reader for an index nobody has checked; `vecGet` remains the reader
# for one a loop has already bounded. The trap is RECOVERABLE -
# `__axiom_recover` answers 77 rather than exiting - so a caller that
# genuinely wants to survive a bad index still can.
#
# WHY NOW: `docs/generics-design.md` section 4. `(-> (Vec a) Int a)`
# cannot answer 0, because there is no way to make up an `a`, and for a
# reference element the 0 is a null the caller dereferences. The
# sentinel had to go before `Vec` could carry an element type.
0.6.4  F  vecGet  answers 0 -> refuses (status 77): a stored zero and a missing element were indistinguishable

# 0.6.4 - `Vec` CARRIES ITS ELEMENT TYPE. `stdlib/Vec.ax` handed out a
# bare `Int` handle; it hands out a parameterised `(Vec a)` now, and
# every public name that takes or answers a vector moved with it. This
# is the port `docs/generics-design.md` section 5 item 5 planned and
# section 4c measured: 4,432 errors to zero.
#
# WHAT A CONSUMER HAS TO DO. The runtime representation did not change
# - a `(Vec a)` is the same machine word the `Int` was - so this is a
# re-TYPING and not a re-implementation. Give the vector an element
# type where you declare one: `(Vec Int)` for machine words (node
# handles, ids, indices), `(Vec String)`, `(Vec (Vec Int))`, or
# `(Vec a)` where the code is genuinely polymorphic. The absent-vector
# sentinel is `(cast (Vec Int) 0)`, and a null test on a handle reads
# `(== (cast Int v) 0)`.
#
# `vecPush` ANSWERS THE VECTOR NOW rather than an `Int`, which is a
# break at every site that discarded the result in `Int` position.
# Write `{ (vecPush v x) 0 }` - never nested, and never in an argument
# position, where `(vecPush acc { ... 0 })` pushes 0 rather than the
# vector.
#
# THE ONE TRAP WORTH WRITING DOWN, because it type-checks and is a
# use-after-free. Do NOT reach for a cast at a `vecPush` argument:
#
#     (vecPush v (cast Int s))     type-checks, LEAKS THE RETAIN
#
# `vecPush`'s element parameter is a type VARIABLE, and
# `docs/memory-model.md` MM-VAL-22 measures that a cast at an argument
# root in a type-variable position classifies that value's evidence 0
# - so `memSetWord`'s `__retainref` emits nothing, the `String`'s share
# is never taken, and the vector holds a pointer to a block that will
# be freed under it. `vecPushStr` and `vecPushVec` are the writes for
# a heterogeneous vector: they take the share EXPLICITLY and pair it
# with the cast, so the count matches a `(Vec String)` push. Reading
# back is `vecGetVec` / `vecGetStr`, and a raw word read as a container
# is `memGetWordVec` / `nodeAVec` / `nodeBVec` / `nodeCVec`. Those six
# are ADDED names and need no declaration of their own; they are named
# here because they are what a consumer hitting this trap needs.
#
# THE FOUR `S` ROWS ARE THE SAME EVENT, not a separate one. A struct
# field that held a vector was declared `Int`, so retyping the field is
# how the container's type reaches the constructor and the accessors.
# No field was added, removed or reordered, and the layout is
# unchanged.
0.6.4  F  vecNew              Int -> (Vec a): the element type is in the handle now
