# (`self_host/parser.ax`'s `parseForExpr`) whose element read is the
# polymorphic `Vec::vecGet`, so the two macros and their three helpers
# are deleted together. A consumer that named a helper directly writes
# `vecLen`, `vecGetStr` or `vecGet`. One that wrote `(for x xs body)`
# writes nothing new - that is the keyword's container shape, and a
# keyword head wins over a macro of the same name, which is why not one
# call site in the tree moved - and `(forInt x nums body)` becomes the
# same `(for x nums body)`, because the keyword reads an element at
# whatever type the `(Vec a)` carries.
