# 0.7.5 - `stdlib/Map.ax` declares its handle. `Map` is a private
# `(struct Map ...)` over the seven-word header `mapAllocTable` already
# built, and the 36 declarations that spelled the handle position `Int`
# spell it `Map`. The layout does not move - struct fields ARE words
# 0..n-1, so `m.len` and `(memGetWord m 0)` are the same load, and
# `tests/stdlib/404-container-reference-maps.ax` still reads the shape
# word 1835024.
#
# WHY IT IS WORTH A BREAK. `Int` is this language's universal heap
# handle and `tests/diagnostics/498-param-through-int.ax` defends it.
# What the convention stops buying is anything at all once two modules'
# handles are the same declared type: `(mapLen internNew)` and
# `(mapGet 12345 1 0)` were well-typed programs, and the first thing
# either did at run time was read a word that was not a map header.
# Both are AX3004 now - tests/diagnostics/483-map-handle-not-int.ax.
#
# THE MUTATORS ANSWER 0, NOT THE HANDLE, and that half is not
# cosmetic. A declared struct is `fldClass` class 2, so codegen retains
# a reference-typed value at every return; a mutator declared
# `(-> Map ... Map)` emits an unpaired retain per call. Measured
# 2026-09-03 on the header's refcount word: 1/1/1 as shipped, 2/2/2
# with the constructor's retain restored, and the reclaim fixture goes
# from 1,359,872 to 87,130,112 bytes of max RSS while printing the same
# answer. A caller that chained on the result passes the handle it
# already holds instead; there were exactly two such sites in this
# tree.
